  urldate = {2023-11-12},
  abstract = {Healthcare information technology (HIT) is on the brink of a paradigm shift: It is expanding to accommodate electronic medical records. But to successfully realize such a critical initiative, healthcare organizations must identify and manage both project risks and organizational risks. This paper examines how organizations can use project management---based on the methods defined in PMI's PMBOK{\textregistered} Guide---to realize initiatives for creating electronic medical records. In doing so, it discusses the natural tension between healthcare professionals and healthcare executives--between scientific tradition and business innovation---in relation to information technology systems. It describes the systematic approach to managing HIT risk, explaining such risk management concepts and techniques as planning and identifying risk, assessing qualitative and quantitative risk, planning risk response, and monitoring and controlling risks related to planning, initiation, scheduling, resources, procurement, quality, communication, training, relationship, budgetary, critical path failure, technology, and acts of God. It outlines six actions for effectively managing a risk strategy. It concludes by recommending a holistic and collaborative approach to managing risk. One appendix shows a sample template for analyzing risk; a second appendix details a risk-identification mind map.},
