  abstract = {{\textexclamdown}h3{\textquestiondown}CONTEXT:{\textexclamdown}/h3{\textquestiondown} {\textexclamdown}p{\textquestiondown}Sport specialization is theorized to increase the risk of sustaining overuse musculoskeletal injuries.{\textexclamdown}/p{\textquestiondown}{\textexclamdown}h3{\textquestiondown}OBJECTIVE:{\textexclamdown}/h3{\textquestiondown} {\textexclamdown}p{\textquestiondown}To complete a systematic review and meta-analysis of the literature to determine if sport specialization is associated with overuse musculoskeletal injuries.{\textexclamdown}/p{\textquestiondown}{\textexclamdown}h3{\textquestiondown}DATA SOURCES:{\textexclamdown}/h3{\textquestiondown} {\textexclamdown}p{\textquestiondown}An electronic search was conducted using the search terms ``specialization,'' ``year-round,'' ``overuse,'' ``repetitive stress,'' ``injury,'' ``young,'' ``pediatric,'' and ``sports.''{\textexclamdown}/p{\textquestiondown}{\textexclamdown}h3{\textquestiondown}STUDY SELECTION:{\textexclamdown}/h3{\textquestiondown} {\textexclamdown}p{\textquestiondown}Studies were included if their population was {$\leq$}18 years of age, if they compared athletes with high or single-sport specialization with athletes with low or multisport specialization, and focused on overuse injuries. {\textexclamdown}/p{\textquestiondown}{\textexclamdown}h3{\textquestiondown}DATA EXTRACTION:{\textexclamdown}/h3{\textquestiondown} {\textexclamdown}p{\textquestiondown}Of the 12 articles that were identified for full-text review, 5 studies met all the inclusion criteria. Four studies provided adequate data for the meta-analysis. Quality scores on the modified Downs and Black scale ranged from 69\% to 81\%.{\textexclamdown}/p{\textquestiondown}{\textexclamdown}h3{\textquestiondown}RESULTS:{\textexclamdown}/h3{\textquestiondown} {\textexclamdown}p{\textquestiondown}Athletes with high specialization were at an increased risk of sustaining an overuse injury compared with athletes with low (pooled relative risk [RR] ratio: 1.81; 95\% confidence interval [CI]: 1.26--2.60) and moderate (pooled RR: 1.18; 95\% CI: 1.05--1.33) specialization. Athletes with moderate specialization were at a higher risk of injury compared with athletes with low specialization (RR: 1.39 [95\% CI: 1.04--1.87]).{\textexclamdown}/p{\textquestiondown}{\textexclamdown}h3{\textquestiondown}LIMITATIONS:{\textexclamdown}/h3{\textquestiondown} {\textexclamdown}p{\textquestiondown}Four of the 5 studies included in this systematic review were included in the meta-analysis because of the lack of access to the original data set for 1 article.{\textexclamdown}/p{\textquestiondown}{\textexclamdown}h3{\textquestiondown}CONCLUSIONS:{\textexclamdown}/h3{\textquestiondown} {\textexclamdown}p{\textquestiondown}Sport specialization is associated with an increased risk of overuse musculoskeletal injuries (Strength of Recommendation Taxonomy grade: B).{\textexclamdown}/p{\textquestiondown}},
  affiliation = {Wisconsin Injury in Sport Laboratory and drbell2@wisc.edu.},
  date-added = {2018-09-07T20:49:19GMT},
