nditions accounted for 52.6\% of the total physical rehabilitation needs worldwide, :{\textexclamdown}i{\textquestiondown}HIV related{\textexclamdown}/i{\textquestiondown} for 5.7\% of the physical rehabilitation needs in low-income nations, but about 1\% in all other locations. Worldwide, significant increases in YLD rates were observed since 1990 for the 6 condition groups ({\textexclamdown}i{\textquestiondown}P{\textexclamdown}/i{\textquestiondown}{\textexclamdown}.01). However, across country types, we observed significant decreases in YLD rates for specific conditions: {\textexclamdown}i{\textquestiondown}pediatric{\textexclamdown}/i{\textquestiondown} in high-income countries, and {\textexclamdown}i{\textquestiondown}neurologic{\textexclamdown}/i{\textquestiondown} and {\textexclamdown}i{\textquestiondown}neoplasm{\textexclamdown}/i{\textquestiondown} conditions in low-income countries ({\textexclamdown}i{\textquestiondown}P{\textexclamdown}/i{\textquestiondown}{\textexclamdown}.01). In upper-middle-income countries, YLD rates from neurologic and neoplasm conditions grew exponentially since 1990, with overall increases of 67\% and 130\%, respectively.{\textexclamdown}/p{\textquestiondown}{\textexclamdown}h3{\textquestiondown}Conclusion{\textexclamdown}/h3{\textquestiondown}{\textexclamdown}p{\textquestiondown}At a global scale, physical rehabilitation needs per capita are growing for all major condition groups, with musculoskeletal and pain conditions currently accounting for over half of those needs. Countries of varying income level have different typologies and evolutionary trends in their rehabilitation needs.{\textexclamdown}/p{\textquestiondown}},
