@article{NewLifeHaldeman1951,
  title = {A New Life for {{Haldeman}}},
  year = {1951},
  month = aug,
  journal = {The Leader-Post},
  pages = {11},
  address = {Regina, Saskatchewan, Canada},
  urldate = {2025-01-24},
  abstract = {anti-inflationary 17,000,-000 1,400,-000; Mountains and jungle, date palms and chickens A new life for Haldeman (Dr. 1 N. Haldeman, who, with his wile and four children left Reglna several months ago to live in South Africa has written the following account of life in that country. Before leaving Re gin a for South Africa we sold everything, shipped the car and other things to Montreal, then the four children and ourselves flew there in our plane. We took the plane apart and crated it and put it on the boat. The 30-day boat trip on the "Thorshall" was very interesting except that I was seasick for the entire journey. Our boat went up the beautiful Saguenay River to Port Alfred before leaving Canada; then on this side we went up the Belgian Congo to Matadi, known as the "Hell Hole" of Africa. We did not know anyone in South Africa but had correspondence with Dr. Blackburn who met us in Capetown. We camped for 10 days at Muizemberg Beach near there, a famous resort where the sand fleas about ate us up, while we put the plane together. Then we all flew up the coast along the garden route to Port Elizabeth where we saw the famous Snake Park, on to Pieter-maritzburg where we spent the weekend with Dr. M. and Dr. A. Peters, retired chiropractors, a very kind elderly couple whose hospitality really made us feel at home i n South Africa; then Ladysmith and Johannesburg. I could not obtain office space in Johannesburg which we did not like so well as it was too much like Chicago or New York, so flew over Pretoria before going back and found that Pretoria was the most beautiful city we had ever seen from the air, so we landed here to look it over. There are long, straight streets, many beautiful parks, flowering trees and shrubs the year round, and long narrow ridges called "koo-pies" from 300 to 700 feet which break up the city and give you a small town or country feeling, although it is a city of 125,000 white people and about an equal number of blacks. We could not obtain office space so we bought a place two and a half miles from the business centre. We have 50,000 square feet. In the front is a sunken rose garden and the roses bloom practically the year round. There are two large date palms, a big fir tree, several jacaranda trees which have purple flowers, beechnut trees, peach trees,' almond trees, a lemon tree and a mulberry tree, poinsettia, a large grape vine arbor which has the nicest grapes we have ever seen, two fish and lily ponds with fountains, large vegetable garden, chicken coop, our own swimming pool and playground, double garage and native quarters. The house is medium size with three large stoeps or verandahs around it. The house divided very nicely into a clinic, leaving three rooms and two August Lush and lovely grows the garden, Lush and lengthy wave the weeds In the wet and washy weather Hoppers' hopping slow recedes; But their squashy, striped backs arching, Army-worms continue marching; Cutworm cuts, and sawfly breeds. Flies and spiders, bugs and beetles, August always has such things. Ants, mosquitoes, flying, biting, Creeping, crawling things with stings. Crickets, cicadas loud rasping, Snakes and lizards boldly basking, Drouth nor deluge respite brings! But, were there neither weeds nor insects. Birds, and fruit, would disappear. Nature's systems interlock; we At our peril interfcr Settlers killed oft prairie-chickens, So their insect-food now thickens, These the fers we fight and fear. GERTRUDE WOOD Glp,n Bain stoeps for living quarters. People here live outside most of the year. In front, across the street, is a very big open park and sports ground and behind us in a "koop-pies" about 700 feet high on which can be seen the residences of the prime minister and the governor-general. Nice back door neighbors to have. Wyn and the children just love it here. The the older children go to school across the park. The place cost us \$1V00 with one-quarter down and \$85 a month payment. We have two native (Negro) garden boys in the summer and one in the winter and a native girl. The law requires that we supply them with separate furnished rooms with nine-inch brick walls, private toilet and shower in fact better living accommodation than 1 enjoyed for most of my life in Canada. We give them food and a lot of their clothing and pay therfl from \$10 to \$15 a month. It is impossible to make a native work hard. It takes three natives to do the work of one white man and the white people here work about half as hard as Canadians. Black labor in South Africa industry is found to be the most expensive labor in the world. The males among the blacks on their reservations do not work at all and the women may work an hour or two so on their reserves their standard of living is not very high, but they are happy and contented with it unless stirred up and stirring them up is almost an impossible job. In spite of the best efforts of the Communists ' they have practically no success. Any talk of a native uprising in Africa is purely Communist wishful t: inking or propaganda. There is no place in the world where the natives are treated better nor receive higher wages than here. Strict law enforcement is necessary to prevent the natives from surrounding territories flocking in here. We have a very good girl, the best we know of, who has been with us since we came. The children like the natives very much tnd the natives would rather play with the small children than do anything else. The natives are very primitive and must not be taken seriously. We get a quite a bang out of them and they are really quite useful. My practice among the whites or Europeans as they are called here, is very good. The South African people are like the people of western Canada. They have been most friendly and helpful. They seem to have as much money as Canadians, professional fees are about the same as In North America and I have found collections to be as good as in Regina. There are only 30 chiropractors for four million white people In Africa. Every morning I have a bunch of natives out back that want to be looked after. With them you must be a chiropractor. At first I would ask the patient what was wrong he . would answer "I am sick." Then I would ask' him "Where does it hurt?" He would answer "All over" and look at me as if I were asking a lot of silly questions. With them 1 can immediately start looking for and removing the ncrv- interference without any unnecessary palaver. The results have been very good. One of the more intelligent natives said "You best doctor in Pretoria. Natives no understand. You no give medicine, native come to doctor sick, get well. No understand." Then he laughed and laughed. A native may be able to read and write in four languages and have no understanding of them, or maybe he just understands what he wants to understand. Some are quite clever in a routine job, but the best of them cannot assume responsibility and will abuse authority. The present government of South Africa knows how to handle the native question. They are endeavoring to make the natives as independent and self- supporting as possible. The Communists and their fellow-travellers continually misrepresent the government's efforts. This includes the international press reports. On May 24 Wyn and 1 and the four children took a four-day motor trip through Kruger Park, spending the first night at Skukuza Camp, the second at Letaba. We left the park at Malopone gate, then drove up through the Forest Reserve, Duivelskloof, through Magoebas Kloof to Pittsburgh and b9ck to Pretoria. Having a servant with us to cook and clean up the camp and help look after the children was an unknown luxury and made camping much more pleasant. The roads compared favorably with those in North America. The 800 mile drive was a continual' change of beauty and interest. The rich farm lands in the beginning of the journey, changed to orchards at Nelspruit, then for miles the picturesque Shangaanii's were doing their dances hoping we would stop and take pictures and give them a few pennies. The tall grass over six feet high hides hundreds of lions; the giraffes looked twice as big as those in the zoo; the funny looking wart hogs' grunts at night made the jungle noises sound like a piggery, broken occasionally by a lion's roar, was a thrilling treat to Canadians. On May 31, another holiday weekend, we made a flight up to the Rhodesias, got lost for three hours over the wilds of Africa, and just managed to get into Salisbury with the gas tanks showing nearly empty after five hours in the air. Wyn had quite a nervous reaction after that. She had been focusing on those gas gauges too much. Stopped overnight in Salisbury, next day had lunch in Lusaka, N. Rhodesia, and dinner in the evening at the Victoria Falls Hotel, S. Rhodesia. Victoria Falls is twice as high as Niagara. The river pours into a gorge. We walked for a mile along the opposite bank from the falls through "The Rain Forest." It gets a continual spray from the falls. The mist from the falls rises up to 1,000 feet. One weekend we flew down to Durban over the "Valley of the Thousand Hills" and back along the Drakensberg Escarpment which is really rough and rugged with snowcapped peaks. ' Africa is a place full of surprises. I .have just been looking after some native patients in a small room fixed up for them. When I approached the group of natives I was greeted by a pleasant cultured English voice. At first I could not discover which one this voice came from, then found it was coming from as black and savage a little native girl as I had ever seen. I tried to have a conversation with her, but she did not have much conversation. One of the other patients, when he took his shirt off, had four two-inch circular .cuts in his back, each with two knife slits in the middle. One of these was healed up with prominent scars and one of the others was fresh. It immediately aroused my curiosity and I asked what this was. They wouldn't answer. Then I said "Did a native doctor do this" and one said "Yes." Then I said "What was he trying to do, let the Kaifir poison out" and they all laughed. Then 1 said "Did it help?" and the answer was !'No". and 1 said "Maybe he didn't push the knives in far enough" then they all laughed again. "Kaffir poison" seems to be a native phrase for being sick. We have seen the native war dances, in fact, our native girl is in Lobola to the scon of a chief and she is good a1 the dances and doing the native rhythms on the drums. Within a few miles you can hear the native drums going night and day. Lobolaa means "contracted for." They will get married when he has finished paying. They have two children, one 12 years old. Within a short flight or short drive there is such a vast variety of things to see that we don't think we will ever get tired of it. There are the tropical jungles, desserts, or the Indian Ocean, we get back In the city there is every modern convenience that we could obtain in Canada, in fact some that cannot be obtained. For instance we went down to the store and bought a frigidaire de luxe. Frigidaires, cars and electrical goods are freely available. Some makes of cars have a waiting list. We see the same movies here, read the same magazines and drink Coco-Cola. The cost of living is much less than in Canada and fresh fruits and vegetables can be obtained the year round as well as the luscious and unusual tropical fruits in season. Everything seems to be booming here, increasing industrial activity and mining prospecting is going ahead very fast. This country seems to have unlimited opportunities for development. The Rhodesias and South Africa could easily stand 50 million white people. We flew over hundreds of miles in which we could scarcely see even a native hut. The climate here is about the same as California except for the altitude, 4,200 feet in Pretoria. Along the east coast there ere many good towns and the climate is similar to Florida except it does not get as hot in summer. We have camped out in various places throughout South Africa. We found it just as safe as camping out on trips in Canada and United States. J. N. HALDEMAN, 259 Zootpansberr Road, Rietondale, Pretoria, South Africa, What makes it turnover? The Danish tippe-top . A Danish engineer's simple and happen?" And the man who at ingenious toy for children has become a mystery for kings and statesmen. "What," they ask, "makes the tippe-top turn over?" The tippe-top is a spherical top about the size of a small apple with a stftjng stem. It is set spinning with a snap of the ftgers and begins to rotate with the stem pointing vertically into the air. Then comes the mystery. The one time n nis Drnnani career rescued atomic physics from the doldrums with his sensational theories replied: "I have tried to fathom it out but I cannot find the solution!" The solution of the tippe-top enigma has become a matter of conscience for the professor. When he was at a graduation ceremony at a Swedish university, attended by King Gustav oscillations change so that the Adolf of Sweden, Professor Bohr stem of the top describes larger had the puzzling toy in his pocket, and larger circles until, with a n nearly spoiled the festivities little jerk, the top turns upside uhen the professor produced it. down and continues to rotate bal- Soon the assembled company were ancing on its stem. amazed to see a king, a cabinet When Mr. Winston Churchill minister and Nobel prizewinner visited Copenhagen last year he engrossed in playing with the top if to passing an to the of "If our Six a for the was introduced to me iippc-top by the Danish atomic physicist, Professor Niels Bohr during a on the floor! Professor Bohr is determined to find the mathematical solution to dinner civen bv Kintf Frederick, the puzzle of the tippe-top. After the top had been spun three If he succeeds he has promised times the inevitable question came to send a report to Mr. Churc