  urldate = {2023-09-08},
  abstract = {It was the 6th Annual Convention of the American Physiotherapy Association in 1927, and a well-known figure, Mary McMillan, was about to give an invited speech on lower back conditions. Before her speech began, McMillan provided the following disclaimer: ``In this talk, I do not make any claim to discuss etiological factors, except in a very general way.''1 She continued with, ``I do want to have you view the situation with me entirely from the physiotherapist's standpoint in order that we can treat such cases intelligently {\dots} and so be of real service to our doctor {\dots} and his patient.''1 At the time, the Association was only 6~years old, and a prescription by a physician was legally required before treatment could be rendered.2 To protect the fledgling profession, leaders carefully cultivated its image and willingly pointed out their dependence on physician expertise. Even the name ``physical therapist'' was apparently chosen to indicate that they only operated under the supervision of physicians.3},
