  publisher = {Elsevier},
  doi = {10.1016/j.spinee.2020.02.015},
  abstract = {{\textexclamdown}h2{\textquestiondown}ABSTRACT{\textexclamdown}/h2{\textquestiondown}{\textexclamdown}h3{\textquestiondown}Background Context{\textexclamdown}/h3{\textquestiondown}{\textexclamdown}p{\textquestiondown}There is often discrepancy between clinical presentation and lumbar magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) findings.{\textexclamdown}/p{\textquestiondown}{\textexclamdown}h3{\textquestiondown}Purpose{\textexclamdown}/h3{\textquestiondown}{\textexclamdown}p{\textquestiondown}The purpose of this study was to assess the relationship of high-intensity zones (HIZs) on MRI with low back pain (LBP), sciatica, and back-related disability.{\textexclamdown}/p{\textquestiondown}{\textexclamdown}h3{\textquestiondown}Study design{\textexclamdown}/h3{\textquestiondown}{\textexclamdown}p{\textquestiondown}Cross-sectional, population-based Southern Chinese cohort study.{\textexclamdown}/p{\textquestiondown}{\textexclamdown}h3{\textquestiondown}Patient sample{\textexclamdown}/h3{\textquestiondown}{\textexclamdown}p{\textquestiondown}Of 1,414 possible participants, data from 1214 participants (453 males, 761 females; mean age of 48.16.3 years) were included.{\textexclamdown}/p{\textquestiondown}{\textexclamdown}h3{\textquestiondown}Outcome Measures{\textexclamdown}/h3{\textquestiondown}{\textexclamdown}p{\textquestiondown}Presence of single-level, homogeneous multi-level (same type HIZs of morphology and topography) and heterogeneous multi-level (mixed type HIZs of morphology and topography) HIZs and other MRI phenotypes were assessed at each level with T2-weighted 3T sagittal MRI of L1-S1. Associations with LBP, sciatica and Oswestry Disability Index were correlated with HIZ profiles.{\textexclamdown}/p{\textquestiondown}{\textexclamdown}h3{\textquestiondown}Results{\textexclamdown}/h3{\textquestiondown}{\textexclamdown}p{\textquestiondown}In all, 718 individuals had HIZs (59.1\%). Disc degeneration/displacement were more prevalent in HIZ individuals (p{\textexclamdown}0.001). HIZ subjects experienced prolonged severe LBP more frequently (39.6\% vs 32.5\%; p{\textexclamdown}0.05) and had higher ODI scores (10.7  13.7 vs 8.9  11.3; p{\textexclamdown}0.05). Posterior multilevel HIZ were significantly associated with prolonged severe LBP (OR:2.18; 95\% CI:1.42-3.37) in comparison to anterior only, anterior/posterior or other patterns of HIZ. Multilevel homogeneous or heterogeneous HIZs were significantly associated with prolonged, severe LBP (OR:1.53 to 1.57; p{\textexclamdown}0.05). Individuals with homogeneous HIZs had a higher risk of sciatica (OR:1.51, p{\textexclamdown}0.05).{\textexclamdown}/p{\textquestiondown}{\textexclamdown}h3{\textquestiondown}Conclusions{\textexclamdown}/h3{\textquestiondown}{\textexclamdown}p{\textquestiondown}This is the first large-scale study to note that lumbar HIZs, and specific patterns therein, are potentially clinically-relevant imaging biomarkers that are independently and significantly associated with prolonged/severe LBP and sciatica. HIZs, especially homogenous multilevel HIZ, should be noted in the global pain imaging phenotype assessment.{\textexclamdown}/p{\textquestiondown}},
  affiliation = {Department of Orthopaedics and Traumatology, The University of Hong Kong, Hong Kong, SAR, China; Spine Care Center, Wakayama Medical University Kihoku Hospital, Wakayama, Japan; Department of Orthopaedic Surgery, Wakayama Medical University, Wakayama, Japan.. Electronic address: m-tera@wakayama-med.ac.jp.},
  date-added = {2020-03-13T21:21:16GMT},
  date-modified = {2022-04-30T18:18:36GMT},
  langid = {english},
  local-url = {file://localhost/Users/nately/Dropbox/Articles/Library.papers3/S/2020/Teraguchi/2020\%20Teraguchi\%20The\%20Spine\%20Journal-1.pdf},
  pmid = {32135303},
